{"text": " STK push is one direction. You ask in a customer to pay. C2B is the other. A customer choosing to pay your business from their emperse menu. B2C is the third. Your business pay money out to any emperse number. Together, these three flows cover every payment use case in East African business. In this lesson, complete C2B implementation, registering validation and confirmation URLs, building both callback handlers and the validation accept slash reject logic. Complete B2C implementation, the security credential, the three command ID types and handling the asynchronous result and queue time out URL callbacks. We cover C2B URL registration, the one time setup that connects your pay bill or till to your callback server. Validation URL versus confirmation URL, what each callback does and when SafariCom calls them. Building a validation endpoint that accepts or rejects payments in real time based on business rules. And B2C security credential, what it is, why it is different from OAuth and how to generate it. C2B has two callbacks. The validation URL is called before SafariCom processes the payment. You can accept or reject it in real time. If you reject it by returning result code not zero, the customer's money is never moved. The eight second validation deadline is the most commonly missed C2B requirement. In production, if your database is on a slow server or you have a complex validation query without proper indexing, the validation URL will time out and SafariCom will silently accept the payment anyway, defeating the purpose of validation entirely. Always index your account reference lookup column and keep the validation handler logic minimal and fast. Remember this, validation URL equals real time decision. Must respond in under eight seconds. Money has not moved yet. Confirmation URL equals notification only. Money has already moved. Cannot be reversed in this callback. Treat them as completely different workflows. Apply what you learn in this lesson and 